ney": -2.35}, "rating_margin": -13.2, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.05, "baseline_margin": -9.46, "analyst_adjustment": -1.04, "key_factors": ["Moore's overwhelming fundraising edge: $2.1M raised and $1.1M on hand versus under $60K for Womack", "R+8 district lean with incumbency and a Solid/Likely R expert consensus across ten raters", "Womack's October felony summons headlines added drag to an already underfunded campaign", "No recent independent polling; the only public poll was March, Republican-sponsored, and showed Moore +8"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, Moore's dominant fundraising, and a unified expert consensus all point to his reelection, and Womack's legal troubles compound her underdog status. Sparse, dated polling keeps uncertainty wide but does not change the direction.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling showing a single-digit race, or a scandal involving Moore, could narrow what is otherwise a comfortable Republican edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tim Moore (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 14th District leans firmly Republican, and incumbent Tim Moore enters the general election with every structural advantage. He has raised roughly $2.1 million and holds more than $1 million on hand, while Democrat LaKesha Womack has raised under $60,000 with less than $5,000 remaining. Outside groups have spent to support Moore, with no comparable spending on the other side. Rating outlets across the spectrum place the race in the Solid or Likely Republican column.\n\nThe campaign's only public poll, conducted in March by a Republican-sponsored firm, showed Moore ahead by eight points among likely voters. October headlines focused on Womack being served a felony summons at a campaign event, an unwelcome distraction for her bid. Prediction markets price her chances at roughly one in four. The forecast expects Moore to